{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEN8bf7kLEmknE6V8gLX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I split the system prompt into two cached blocks, but the fix may not take effect yet. The first block might be below Sonnet 4.5's 1024-token minimum cache length, which would make the breakpoint a no-op. I couldn't confirm that offline.\n\n**What changed**\n- `bot.py`, `build_system`: the instructions are now their own block with a `cache_control` marker. The per-athlete context (date, profile, zones, goal, facts) is a second marked block. A `save_fact` now only invalidates the second block.\n- `README.md`: the caching paragraph describes the two blocks.\n- `ANSWER.md`: the cause, the change, the open risk, and which parts of the colleague's note applied.\n\nI checked offline that the static block is byte-identical after a fact is added and on the next day, and that the volatile block changes. I did not make an API call.\n\n**Open risk**\nMy estimate (chars/4, not a real token count) puts the static prefix (core tools plus instructions) at about 730 tokens. That may be under the 1024-token minimum. If it is, Anthropic silently skips the first breakpoint, and a memory write still evicts everything. Checking this needs one live call: send a message, then one with a `save_fact`, and look at `cache_creation_input_tokens` in `logs/usage.jsonl`. If it is about the size of the volatile block (~250 tokens), the fix works. If it is about the whole prompt, the instructions need to be longer. Padding them is a product decision, so I did not do it.\n\n**Not changed**\nThe keyword tool groups also cause misses. Tools sit above the system prompt in the cache prefix, so a message that adds the `plan` group evicts the cache. This is a separate cost from memory writes, and I left it for you to decide.\n\nNothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_